PROPOSED CHANGE — NOT APPLIED. For review only. Apply only after the Neon password rotation is confirmed.

Purpose: stop hard-coding the Flyway Maven plugin's database URL, user and password in pom.xml. The three
values come from environment variables with NO default. If a variable is unset, Maven leaves the literal
text ${env.NAME} in place, so `mvn flyway:*` fails with an obvious connection error instead of silently
using any database.

The three removed lines hold real values, so they are shown redacted here — this file must never contain them.
Only backend/pom.xml lines 123-125 change. application-local.yml is not touched.

--- a/backend/pom.xml
+++ b/backend/pom.xml
@@ -120,9 +120,9 @@
 				<artifactId>flyway-maven-plugin</artifactId>
 				<version>12.4.0</version>
 				<configuration>
-					<url>[REDACTED: jdbc url with host and database name]</url>
-					<user>[REDACTED: database owner user]</user>
-					<password>[REDACTED: database password]</password>
+					<url>${env.FLYWAY_DB_URL}</url>
+					<user>${env.FLYWAY_DB_USER}</user>
+					<password>${env.FLYWAY_DB_PASSWORD}</password>
 					<locations>
 						<location>filesystem:src/main/resources/db/migration</location>
 					</locations>

Notes
- The variable names are dedicated (FLYWAY_DB_*), deliberately different from SPRING_DATASOURCE_* (the app) and
  NEON_BRANCH_* (the branch test), so nothing is picked up by accident.
- No value, default or fallback appears anywhere in the diff.
- How it would be applied after your review: a line-based replacement of lines 123-125, guarded by a structural
  check that those three lines are the <url>, <user> and <password> elements, so a shifted file is refused
  rather than mis-edited. The old lines are never printed or written to a file.
- Rotation does not remove the old password from git history (it is on origin/main, origin/dev and other branches).
  Rotating makes it harmless; a history rewrite is a separate decision.
